¶
'''yellowroot''' (yel′ō-röt), ''n.'' '''1.''' Same as ''shrub-yellowroot.''—'''2.''' An American herb, ''Hydrastis Canadensis'', named also ''orange-root'', ''yellow puccoon'', ''Indian paint'', ''turmeric-root'', and especially (in medicine) ''goldenseal.'' {{smaller|Its rootstock contains hydrastine and berberine, and is an officinal remedy of an unquestioned tonic property and with various powers less settled, applied in dyspepsia, in jaundice and other disorders of the liver, as a laxative, alterative, etc. See ''Hydrastis'' and ''hydrastine.''—'''Shrub yellowroot.''' See ''Xanthorrhiza'' and ''shrub-yellowroot.''}}

'''yellowrump''' (yel′ō-rump), ''n.'' The yellow-rumped warbler, ''Dendrœca coronata'', the yellow-crowned warbler, or myrtle-bird. See ''warbler'' and ''myrtle-bird.''—{{smaller|'''Western yellowrump''', Audubon’s warbler, ''Dendrœca auduboni.'' See ''warbler.''}}

'''yellow-rumped''' (yel′ō-rumpt), ''a.'' Having the rump (or upper tail-coverts in some cases) yellow, as various birds. (See ''yellowrump.'') The yellow-rumped seed-eater is a certain finch, ''Crithagra chrysopyga.''

'''yellow-sally''' (yel′ō-sal″i), ''n.'' See ''yellow sally'', under ''sally''<sup>3</sup>, 2.

'''yellowseed''' (yel′ō-sēd), ''n.'' A species of peppergrass, ''Lepidum campestre'', native in the Old World, introduced in North America; mithri-

