'''yellow-wood''' (yel′ō-wu̇d), ''n.'' '''1.''' Same as ''fustic.''—'''2.''' ''Cladrastis tinctoria'', the American or Kentucky yellow-wood, in cultivation commonly known as ''Virgilia lutea'', also called ''gopher-wood'' and ''yellow ash.'' {{smaller|In the wild state it is a rare tree, found locally in Kentucky, Tennessee, and}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Yellow-wood (''Cladrastis tinctoria''). ''a'', pod.}}}}
{{smaller|North Carolina. It grows from 30 to 45 feet high, and bears pinnate leaves with seven to ten leaflets, and ample racemes of white pea-like flowers drooping from the ends of the branches. It is highly ornamental for both flowers and foliage. It has a hard yellow wood, which is used for fuel and to some extent for gun-stocks, and yields a clear yellow dye. For another American yellow-wood, see ''Schæfferia''. The Osage orange, ''Maclura aurantiaca'', of the same genus as the fustic, is sometimes so named, as is also the shrub-yellowroot, ''Xanthorrhiza apiifolia.''}} '''3.''' Same as ''white teak.'' See ''teak.''—'''Australian yellow-wood.''' {{smaller|See ''light yellow-wood'' and ''Queensland yellow-wood. Acronychia lævis'', of the ''Rutaceæ'', found at Moreton Bay, is also called ''yellow-wood'', as are ''Hovea lon-''}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''yellow-wort''' (yel′ō-wėrt), ''n.'' A European annual plant, ''Chlora perfoliata'', of the gentian family. {{smaller|It is a very glaucous plant, about a foot high, the stem-leaves in pairs and connate-perfoliate, the flowers bright-yellow in loose terminal cymes. Also called ''yellow centaury.''}}

'''yellow-wrack''' (yel′ō-rak), ''n.'' A seaweed, ''Ascophyllum nodosum'' (''Fucus nodosus'' of Linnæus).

'''yellowy''' (yel′ọ̄-i), ''a.'' [< ''yellow'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Somewhat yellow; yellowish; flavescent.
{{smaller block|A little kerchief of cobweb muslin and ancient ''yellowy'' lace . . . is “Over her decent shoulders drawn.”
{{right|''R. Broughton'', Joan, ii. 2.}}}}

'''yelm''' (yelm), ''n.'' [< ME. *''ȝelm'', < AS. ''gelm'', ''gilm'', a handful. Cf. ''glean''<sup>1</sup>.] A handful; a sheaf of straw or grain. [Prov. Eng.]

'''yelm''' (yelm), ''v. t.'' and ''i.'' [< ''yelm'', ''n.''] To lay straw in order fit for use by a thatcher. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]
{{smaller block|A woman ''yelming'' 14 days, 1s. 9d.
{{right|''H. Hall'', Society in Elizabethan Age, App. II.}}}}

'''yelp''' (yelp), ''v. i.'' [Also dial. ''yaup'', ''yawp''; < ME. ''yelpen'', ''ȝelpen'', boast, < AS. ''gilpan'', ''gielpan'', ''gylpan'' (pret. ''gealp'') (MHG. ''gelfen''), boast, exult, = Icel. ''gjālpa'', yelp; perhaps ult. akin to ''yell''. The mod. sense ‘yelp’ as a dog is prob. due to Scand. Cf. ''yawp''.] '''1†.''' To boast; cry up a thing; exult; brag.
{{smaller block|This zenne is ybounde ine than [the one] thet be his oȝene mouthe him ''yelpth'' other of his wytte, other of his kenne, other of his workes. {{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'', p. 22.}}

I kepe noght of armes for to ''yelpe.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight’s Tale, l. 1380.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>Let the wild
Lean-headed Eagles ''yelp'' alone.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, vii.}}}}
{{smaller block|Now a hen ''yelps'' on the other side, and he [a turkey-cock] pauses between the two calls, then struts and gobbles again. {{right|''Sport with Rod and Gun'', II. 762.}}}}

'''yelp''' (yelp), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yelp'', ''ȝelp'', < AS. ''gielp'', ''gylp'', boast; from the verb.] '''1†.''' A boast; boasting.—'''2.''' An eager bark or cry; a sharp, quick bark or cry caused by fear or pain.
{{smaller block|<poem>The dog
With inward ''yelp'' and restless forefoot plies
His function of the woodland.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lucretius.}}}}
{{smaller block|He put the dog’s nose in and patted him, and Spike gave a ''yelp'', as if a rat were in prospect.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Kit and Kitty, xxiv.}}}}

'''yelper''' (yel′pėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yelpere''; < ''yelp'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who boasts; a boaster.
{{smaller block|The ''yelpere'' is the cockou, thet ne kan naȝt zinge bote of him-zelue. {{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'', p. 22.}}}}
'''2.''' One who or that which yelps. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') A young dog; a whelp. ''Halliwell.'' (''b'') In ''ornith.'': (1) The avoset, ''Recurvirostra avocetta'': so called from its cry. [Local, Eng.] (2) The greater yellowlegs, ''Totanus melanoleucus. Shore Birds'', p. 37. (''c'') A whistle or call used by sportsmen to imitate the cry of the wild turkey-hen.}}
{{smaller block|We now take our ''yelper'', and give a few sharp yelps; he [a wild turkey] hears the call.
{{right|''Sport with Rod and Gun'', II. 762.}}}}

'''yelping''' (yel′ping), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yelping'', ''ȝulping''; verbal n. of ''yelp'', ''v.''] '''1†.''' Boasting.
{{smaller block|The uerthe [fourth], . . . whereby the proude sseaweth prede of his herte is ''yelpingge''. ''Ayenbite of Inwyt'', p. 22.}}
